<p>The Bench addressed the obvious tension directly: it was mindful that one co-accused was being denied bail in the very judgment granting it to another, and explained that the allegations against the two were &ldquo;quite distinct&rdquo; — A-2 prima facie instrumental in furthering the larger conspiracy and harbouring accused persons, A-15&rsquo;s role not discernible at this stage despite the alleged network around him.</p>

<h2>The legal framework the Court worked with</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Principle</th><th>Application</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 43D(5) UAPA — bail barred where accusations are prima facie true</td><td>Satisfied against A-2; not satisfied against A-15, where no substantial material or corroboration had emerged</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Watali — prima facie means on the face of the chargesheet material</td><td>The Court examined the chargesheets and protected-witness statements accused-wise rather than treating the module case as monolithic</td></tr>
  <tr><td>K.A. Najeeb — constitutional courts may grant bail on Article 21 grounds despite the statutory bar</td><td>Invoked for A-15: prolonged custody with no charge framed and trial distant can override the embargo</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Protected-witness sensitivity</td><td>For A-2, the unexamined protected witnesses were the reason for refusal — and the trigger for the renewal liberty after their examination or one year</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The renewal clock: a notable drafting device</h2>
<p>Rather than leaving the refused appellant to an indefinite horizon, the Bench built a timeline into the order: upon completion of the protected witnesses&rsquo; examination or the expiry of one year from the judgment, whichever is earlier, A-2 may renew his bail plea, to be considered on its own merits uninfluenced by the earlier orders. The device — increasingly common in UAPA matters — converts a refusal into a review point, disciplining the prosecution&rsquo;s trial pace without releasing an accused the court considers presently dangerous.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Disaggregate the chargesheet.</strong> In module prosecutions, the strongest defence work maps each allegation to each accused; the 43D(5) assessment is individual, and generalised conspiracy narratives do not transfer automatically.</p>
  <p><strong>Track what remains unsubstantiated.</strong> The deletion-of-evidence allegation failed for want of record support — an appellate bench will notice the gap if counsel shows it.</p>
  <p><strong>Use the renewal liberty.</strong> Where refusal comes with a witness-completion or time trigger, diarise it; the renewed plea stands on a changed footing by court design.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. It is not legal advice; bail in UAPA matters turns on the material specific to each accused.</div>
